One common way to introduce vulnerabilities is through **Insecure Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize untrusted data from a user (such than via a cookie or a POST parameter), an attacker can craft a malicious byte stream to execute arbitrary code on the server.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class VulnerableServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(8080);
        System.out.println("Server listening on port 8080...");

        while (true) {
            try (Socket socket = serverSocket.accept();
                 ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {

                // VULNERABLE: Deserializing data directly from an untrusted network socket
                // An attacker can send a crafted gadget chain to achieve Remote Code Execution (RCE).
                Object obj = ois.readObject();
                System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.toString());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

